        <body>
            <div xml:id="d386">

                <pb facs="1146" n="1140" xml:id="pg1140"/>

                <head>No. 39.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Macfarlane to Mr. Blount.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1893-12-31T23:59:59-10:30"
                            notBefore="1893-07-16T00:00:00-10:30"
                    >undated</date>.]</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Blount</hi>: I send you the copies of the
                    lottery petitions duly attested by the clerk of the Legislative Assembly (C. J.
                    McCarthy), whose certificate I have been waiting for all this time. This is my
                    reason for not sending these lists before.</p>
                <p>Accompanying these lists I send you another list showing the names of those who
                    are connected with the Provisional Government, also a list of prominent
                    mechanics. Both of these lists are taken from among the signers of the lottery
                    petitions.</p>
                <p>Also find affidavit of Phillips and Rooney, who certify to the genuineness of the
                    signatures. Mr. Phillips was the president of the Mechanics’ Union and a noble
                    of the Legislature of 1890. Mr. Rooney has been for many years a sugar
                    plantation manager.</p>
                <p>Very respectfully,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. C. Macfarlane.</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>His Excellency <hi rendition="#small-caps">J. H. Blount.</hi></p>
                </postscript>











